{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c53f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06f78-0ca7-7650-b043-6178918d4030"},"sequence":250,"recorded_at":1780531400000495,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"09066c51-cf5e-4ab0-8fb1-8339d1838d54","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"09066c51-cf5e-4ab0-8fb1-8339d1838d54","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"09066c51-cf5e-4ab0-8fb1-8339d1838d54"},"call_id":"call_01a06f78a4d67dc0866f089933b3e671","text":"Read text file `.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1snnef/TASK-260830-1snnef_review-verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-1snnef review verdict — round 1\n2|\n3|**Verdict: changes requested → `to-dev`.**\n4|\n5|CR `CR-TASK-260830-1snnef-1` rev 1, base `57afcc6`, candidate tree\n6|`f9e66a3c8ea25af155356ba24e2c0e815eef49d3`. I verified the tree I measured **is**\n7|the candidate: after every probe below, a scratch-index `git write-tree` returned\n8|`f9e66a3c8ea25af155356ba24e2c0e815eef49d3` exactly.\n9|\n10|The leaf is strong. The derived inventory is real, load-bearing, and fails closed\n11|in the ways the round-3 verdict asked for. Four findings block acceptance, all\n12|small and all in the same class: a claim measured by a sample presented as a\n13|closure.\n14|\n15|---\n16|\n17|## G-A — attacking the attacker\n18|\n19|### The ratio, denominator established independently\n20|\n21|The derivation under test walks `*.go` (non-test) `FuncDecl` bodies. I built the\n22|denominator two ways that never touch it:\n23|\n24|| Method | What it counts | Result |\n25|| --- | --- | ---: |\n26|| Separate Go AST program: whole-file `ast.Inspect`, position-span exclusion of the two constructors | `&Error{}` composite literals + direct `mismatchf`/`integrityFailure` calls | 75 + 118 = **193** |\n27|| Regex scan of `Detail: \"…\"` and constructor string args | literal-detail sites (blind to the dynamic funnel) | 192 + 1 funnel = **193** |\n28|| The inventory's declared table | `declaredRefusalArms` rows + funnel row | 192 + 1 = **193** |\n29|\n30|**193 of 193 arms derived and declared — 100%**, with the denominator agreeing\n31|across three independent methods. The same program reported\n32|`outsideFuncDecl=0` and constructor identifier occurrences `= calls + 2\n33|declarations`, i.e. the two blind spots below are **currently empty**, measured,\n34|not assumed.\n35|\n36|### Nine probes against the derivation\n37|\n38|Copy-aside, `shasum`-verified restore after each.\n39|\n40|| # | Shape | Expectation | Result |\n41|| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n42|| A1 | arm in a package-level `var f = func() *Error {…}`, called from `CheckReplicable` | should fail | **SURVIVED — blind spot** |\n43|| A2 | `refuse := mismatchf; return refuse(\"planted alias arm\")` | should fail | **SURVIVED — blind spot** |\n44|| A3 | arm in a brand-new production file in the package | should fail | KILLED (both directions) |\n45|| A4 | arm in a func literal *nested inside* a `FuncDecl` body | should fail | KILLED (both directions) |\n46|| A5 | arm on a method of a new type | should fail | KILLED (both directions) |\n47|| A6 | `Detail:` from a package const identifier | should fail closed | FATAL, \"not a static clause\" |\n48|| A7 | delete an existing arm | reverse direction | KILLED |\n49|| A8 | rename an existing detail | both directions | KILLED |\n50|| A9 | arms built through a **new** helper not in `constructorFunctions` | should fail closed | FATAL, \"not a static clause\" |\n51|\n52|7 of 9 killed or fail-closed. A9 is the important positive: a producer cannot\n53|route refusals through a fresh helper to hide them — the helper's own dynamic\n54|`Detail` fatals the derivation.\n55|\n56|### Is the declared side a closed loop against itself?\n57|\n58|No, and this is worth saying plainly because the sibling Story's round 2 found\n59|exactly that failure. The table is hand-written, but it is anchored to **three**\n60|things the table cannot edit: the derivation (bijection, both directions), the\n61|production `Code` const values (`TestDeclaredRefusalWiresMatchProductionConsts`),\n62|and real test bodies indexed from the test AST (`TestDeclaredRefusalTestsResolve`).\n63|Truncating the derivation fails the reverse direction (A3, A7 confirm). The\n64|numerator and denominator do not come from the same literal.\n65|\n66|The residual weakness is different and narrower: when the derivation is **blind**\n67|to a shape, both directions stay silent because neither side ever sees the arm.\n68|A1 and A2 are that case.\n69|\n70|### Does it cover `conformance.go`'s 989 new lines?\n71|\n72|Yes. The glob is file-level and A3 proves a new file is scanned. Of the 193 arms,\n73|40 live in `conformance.go` (34 `&Error{}` + 6 `mismatchf`), all declared.\n74|\n75|---\n76|\n77|## G-A finding (blocking)\n78|\n79|### F1 — README states a capability two probes falsify\n80|\n81|`README.md:1983` (new text):\n82|\n83|> \"an AST-derived refusal-arm inventory (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`) that\n84|> requires **every** production refusal arm to resolve to a named asserting test\n85|> in both directions, **so a refusal that ships unwitnessed fails the suite**\"\n86|\n87|A1 and A2 each ship a reachable production refusal arm with a brand-new detail\n88|and leave both inventory tests, `go vet`, `gofmt` and all 14 packages green. The\n89|claim is a closure claim; the guard covers a subset. The file's own \"Stated\n90|bounds\" block lists three bounds and does not name this one, while its\n91|`deriveRefusalArms` comment advertises the file-level closure (\"so a new\n92|production file is scanned without anyone remembering it\") without the\n93|declaration-level bound beside it.\n94|\n95|DoD item: \"README/doctor/capability evidence … updated **without unsupported\n96|claims**.\"\n97|\n98|Fix is cheap and I'd prefer the real one over a caveat: walk `file.Decls` for\n99|`GenDecl`/`ValueSpec` func literals as well (or `ast.Inspect` the whole file with\n100|a constructor-span exclusion, as my audit program does), and fatal when\n101|`mismatchf`/`integrityFailure` appears as an `Ident` outside call position. Both\n102|are ~10 lines and both blind spots are empty today, so neither changes the table.\n103|\n104|---\n105|\n106|## G-B — the whole Story, re-run against this tree\n107|\n108|I re-ran **all four inherited batteries** against this exact candidate.\n109|\n110|| Battery | Applied | Killed | Survived | Anchor missing |\n111|| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n112|| Leaf 1 review-3 (`review3-mutant-definitions.json`, 71) | 70 | 61 | 9 | 1 (N12) |\n113|| Leaf 1 review-2 (`review2-mutant-definitions.json`, 60) | 59 | 54 | 5 | 1 (N12) |\n114|| Leaf 2 battery-1 (60) | 58 | 58 | **0** | 2 |\n115|| Leaf 2 battery-2 (47) | 47 | 47 | **0** | 0 |\n116|\n117|**Zero resurrections.** Every mutant leaf 1 and leaf 2 recorded as KILLED is\n118|still KILLED here. Two improvements: leaf 1's declared survivor **X03** is now\n119|killed, and leaf 2's declared survivor **M92** is genuinely killed by this leaf's\n120|new `TestAdmitProbeRefusesNilRegistry` (I verified the failing-test list, not just\n121|the exit code).\n122|\n123|Leaf 1's survivor set reproduces exactly: N09, N13, N14, N15, X01, X02, X04, X10.\n124|`T01` was \"not applied\" in leaf 1's record; its anchor applies now, and when the\n125|traceability packages are included it is **KILLED** by six `tracecheck` tests —\n126|so this leaf's +57-line `ownership.v0.5.0.json` change has not weakened the\n127|self-minted-declaration gate.\n128|\n129|### G-B bound worth recording: the inventory inflates future kill counts\n130|\n131|M71, M72 and M95 report as KILLED in a re-run **solely** because the two inventory\n132|tests fail — the failing-test list contains nothing else. The inventory kills any\n133|arm-deleting mutant by construction, so leaf-2-style mutation scores measured\n134|after this leaf are not comparable to leaf 2's 91/95. Nothing behavioral changed\n135|for those three. The producer's own bounds statement is honest here (\"M71/M72 …\n136|unknown; M92/M95 stay survivors\") — credit — but the tree carries no warning for\n137|the next person who re-runs a battery.\n138|\n139|---\n140|\n141|## G-B finding (blocking) — the harness in the permissive direction\n142|\n143|`conformance.go` asserts things about other code. Twelve narrowing mutants on it,\n144|copy-aside, restore-verified. Nine killed. Three survivors, all on gates the AC\n145|names directly, none declared in the leaf's evidence:\n146|\n147|### F2 — `CheckErrorAllowed`: the refusal set is a sample, not a complement\n148|\n149|Mutant P20: admit `terminal_backend_unavailable` for **every** operation, ahead of\n150|the table lookup. **Whole suite green.** The production table omits\n151|`CodeUnavailable` for `quiesce-input`, `wait-safe-boundary`, `request-stop` and\n152|`terminate-stale`, so the mutant makes the harness pass a backend emitting an\n153|error code the contract forbids for that operation.\n154|\n155|`TestCheckErrorAllowedRefusesEveryUnlistedCode` hand-picks 3–5 refused codes per\n156|operation. The name claims \"every unlisted code\"; it measures a sample. The\n157|package already pins the catalog error vocabulary, so the complement is derivable:\n158|for each operation, assert every catalogued code **not** in its allowed set is\n159|refused. That is the difference between a sample and a closure, and this is a\n160|conformance harness — permissive is the direction that matters.\n161|\n162|### F3 — `TestCheckAttachResultRequiresTripleEquality` measures a pair\n163|\n164|Mutant P8: drop the `resultInputAuthorized != auth.InputAuthorized` conjunct.\n165|**Whole suite green.** The test builds one `auth` with `InputAuthorized: true`\n166|and checks `(false,true)` and `(true,false)`; the case only the third leg catches\n167|— `(false,false)` against `auth{true}` — is never driven. The test name asserts\n168|the property; nothing measures it. One line fixes it.\n169|\n170|### F4 — attach expiry boundary instant unwitnessed\n171|\n172|Mutant P16: `!now.Before(expires)` → `now.After(expires)`, i.e. admit attach at\n173|exactly the expiry instant. **Whole suite green.** The expiry case uses\n174|`now + 48h`. Production chose refuse-at-`expires`; nothing pins that choice, so\n175|the boundary can flip silently. One case at `now == expires` fixes it.\n176|\n177|### Survivors I am **not** blocking on\n178|\n179|- **P11** — `TranslateLegacyBackend` with an extra escape name (`\"screen\"`)\n180|  survives; the refusal sample is 10 names over an unbounded string domain. Not\n181|  closable by sampling; the closed-set property (`legacyForward` has exactly two\n182|  entries) is the right pin if anyone wants it. Reported as a bound.\n183|- **P13** — `ProjectToLegacy` skipping `ParseID` for the empty ID still refuses,\n184|  with a different wire code. Near-equivalent.\n185|\n186|Killed in the same battery: P1 (drop an op from `idempotencyKeySegments` — the\n187|empty-key permissive path), P4/P5/P7/P10/P14/P15/P17/P18/P19. P15 (`ImportLedger`\n188|treating a malformed image as an empty table — the failed-read-as-absence shape)\n189|is killed both behaviorally **and** by both inventory directions.\n190|\n191|---\n192|\n193|## G-C — bounds\n194|\n195|| Bound | Status after this leaf |\n196|| --- | --- |\n197|| M71/M72 (JCS) unprovable, reported **unknown** | Unchanged behaviorally. They now *read* as killed in a re-run, by the inventory alone. See the G-B bound above. |\n198|| M92/M95 counted as survivors | **M92 is now genuinely killed** (`TestAdmitProbeRefusesNilRegistry`). M95 unchanged behaviorally; reads as killed by the inventory alone. |\n199|| Leaf 2: 19 of 88 executed sites share a detail (69 file-unique) | **Changed, and got worse in absolute terms.** Over all 192 literal-detail arm sites: 20 distinct details cover 82 sites; 110 sites are detail-unique. `conformance.go` adds repeats deliberately (`entrypoint session binding` ×3, `idempotency ledger image` ×4, `idempotency key shape` ×4 across two functions). The occurrence index keeps *rows* distinct; textual test attribution still cannot separate site #1 from #2 in one function, which the file discloses. |\n200|\n201|### Does the derived inventory make leaf 2's \"five vs six\" drift impossible?\n202|\n203|**For that exact statistic, yes — it is now derived, not hand-counted.** I\n204|measured it independently: of 130 distinct details across 192 sites, **zero** have\n205|no textual hit in any test file. That class of manual-enumeration drift is closed.\n206|\n207|**A weaker cousin survives.** `rowResolves` falls back to entry-mention +\n208|wire-code/predicate-mention when the detail is not mentioned, so a row can resolve\n209|while pointing at a test that exercises a *sibling* site sharing its detail. Live\n210|example: `CheckTransition`'s `\"operation vocabulary\"` arm is unreachable\n211|(`ParseOperation` admits exactly the ten operations `transitionTable` covers), it\n212|carries no `bound`, and it resolves through a test that drives `ParseOperation`'s\n213|own arm with the same string. Compare the five re-parse arms, which *do* declare\n214|`boundDefensiveReparse` and are pinned as exactly five by\n215|`TestDefensiveBoundsAreExactlyThese`. Same dead-by-construction class, different\n216|treatment. Not blocking; worth one row or one bound line.\n217|\n218|---\n219|\n220|## Gates I ran myself\n221|\n222|| Gate | Result |\n223|| --- | --- |\n224|| `go test ./... -count=1` | **exit 0**, 14/14 packages ok |\n225|| `go test ./internal/terminalbackend/ -race -count=1` | **exit 0** |\n226|| `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n227|| `gofmt -l .` | clean |\n228|| `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` | exit 0, `acceptance_cases=76`, `bindings=49 … clauses_discharged=17/403` (section coverage unchanged — the ownership delta claims one acceptance case and no new section binding) |\n229|| `go test ./internal/terminalbackend/ -cover` | **89.9%** |\n230|| Candidate tree identity after all probes | `f9e66a3c8ea25af155356ba24e2c0e815eef49d3` ✓ |\n231|\n232|Nit, not a finding: the leaf's evidence and LOGBOOK say \"90.0% of statements\"; I\n233|measure 89.9% on this tree. Round it or restate it.\n234|\n235|Architecture fit is good: the harness holds no authority, does no I/O, keeps\n236|capability gating in `manifest.go` and trust in `terminalbackend.go`, and the\n237|ownership delta adds one acceptance case with twelve real test declarations and\n238|claims no new section binding. Stated bound: nothing outside\n239|`internal/terminalbackend` calls these entry points — by design per §4.B, and\n240|correct for M0, but it does mean \"production entry point\" here means the harness's\n241|own exported surface, not an `ax` call site.\n242|\n243|---\n244|\n245|## What acceptance needs\n246|\n247|1. **F1** — close the two derivation blind spots (preferred) or state them in the\n248|   \"Stated bounds\" block **and** qualify the README sentence. The README claim as\n249|   written is falsified by A1/A2.\n250|2. **F2** — derive `CheckErrorAllowed`'s refusal cases as the complement of each\n251|   operation's allowed set over the pinned catalog vocabulary, and kill P20.\n252|3. **F3** — add `CheckAttachResult(false, false, auth{InputAuthorized: true})`\n253|   and kill P8.\n254|4. **F4** — add the `now == expires` attach case and kill P16.\n255|5. Report the survivor set for `conformance.go` as a ratio, including anything you\n256|   decline to close (P11 and P13 are fine as declared bounds).\n257|\n258|Optional, cheap, and it would make the next re-run honest: one bound line noting\n259|that the inventory kills every arm-deleting mutant by construction, so post-leaf-3\n260|mutation scores are not comparable to leaf 2's 91/95.\n261|\n262|The Story's other two leaves re-measure clean against this tree. Once these four\n263|land, this leaf is ready.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
